Gas Leak Detection System

An ESP32-based embedded system that monitors air quality and environmental conditions, displaying real-time status on an LCD screen. Built as part of an ongoing IoT project.

Overview

The system reads gas concentration, temperature, and humidity, then cycles the readings on a 16x2 I2C LCD. It's designed to eventually log state changes to Firebase and expose that data through a companion Android app.

Hardware

- ESP32 (NodeMCU-32S)
- MQ5 gas sensor (analog output)
- DHT11 temperature and humidity sensor
- 16x2 I2C LCD (address 0x27)

Wiring

MQ5 analog out - GPIO 34
DHT11 data - GPIO 4
LCD SDA - GPIO 21
LCD SCL - GPIO 22

Current Status

Gas sensor (MQ5) and DHT11 readings are taken every 2 seconds.
LCD cycles between two screens every 3 seconds:
- Air Quality: "Normal air quality" / "Gaz detected" (threshold-based on MQ5 reading)
- Temp / Humidity: live DHT11 readings
All readings are also printed to Serial (115200 baud) for debugging.
Project built and managed with PlatformIO.


Libraries

marcoschwartz/LiquidCrystal_I2C
adafruit/DHT sensor library
adafruit/Adafruit Unified Sensor

Next Steps

Firebase integration: connect the ESP32 to WiFi and push live sensor data (state changes only, not raw continuous values) to Firebase Realtime Database.
Android app: build out the Android Studio app (Java) to read live data from Firebase and display current air quality status ("Normal Air Quality" / "Gaz Detected") / the temperature and humidity in a simple interface.
Move WiFi/Firebase credentials out of hardcoded defines into a gitignored config before making the repo public.